DAY 4 – CONTROL STATEMENTS IN JAVA

TYPES OF CONTROL STATEMENTS IN JAVA

Java control statements are used to control the flow of execution
of a program. By default, Java executes code sequentially
from top to bottom. Control statements allow the program
to make decisions, repeat tasks, and jump to different parts
of the code.

Java provides three main types of control statements:

1. Decision Making Statements
2. Looping Statements
3. Jump Statements


--1. DECISION MAKING STATEMENTS--

Decision making statements are used to execute code
based on conditions. A condition is an expression
that evaluates to either true or false.

**IF STATEMENT**

The if statement is used when a block of code
needs to be executed only if a given condition is true.
If the condition is false, the block is skipped.
Important Points:
- Condition must return boolean value
- Used for single-condition checking

Real-world example:
Checking voting eligibility


class IfStatementProgram {

    void checkVotingEligibility() {
        int age = 20;

        if (age >= 18) {
            System.out.println("Eligible to vote");
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        IfStatementProgram obj = new IfStatementProgram();
        obj.checkVotingEligibility();
    }
}


**IF-ELSE STATEMENT**

The if-else statement is used when there are exactly
two possible outcomes. One block executes if the
condition is true and the other executes if it is false.

Important Points:
- Only one block executes
- Improves clarity in decision making

Real-world example:
Checking even or odd number

class IfElseStatementProgram {
    void checkEvenOdd() {
        int number = 9;
        if (number % 2 == 0) {
            System.out.println("Even number");
        } else {
            System.out.println("Odd number");
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        IfElseStatementProgram obj = new IfElseStatementProgram();
        obj.checkEvenOdd();
    }
}



**ELSE-IF LADDER**

The else-if ladder is used when multiple conditions
need to be tested one after another.

Conditions are checked from top to bottom.
The first condition that becomes true is executed.

Important Points:
- Order of conditions is important
- Only one block executes

Real-world example:
Student grading system

class ElseIfLadderProgram {
  void calculateGrade() {
        int marks = 78;
        if (marks >= 90) {
            System.out.println("Grade A");
        } else if (marks >= 75) {
            System.out.println("Grade B");
        } else if (marks >= 60) {
            System.out.println("Grade C");
        } else {
            System.out.println("Fail");
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ElseIfLadderProgram obj = new ElseIfLadderProgram();
        obj.calculateGrade();
    }
}



**SWITCH STATEMENT**

The switch statement is a multi-branch decision
making statement. It executes code based on the
value of a variable or expression.

Important Points:
- Used for fixed number of choices
- break prevents fall-through
- default executes when no case matches

Real-world example:
Menu-driven applications

class SwitchStatementProgram {

    void menuExample() {
        int choice = 2;

        switch (choice) {
            case 1:
                System.out.println("Create Account");
                break;
            case 2:
                System.out.println("Login");
                break;
            case 3:
                System.out.println("View Profile");
                break;
            default:
                System.out.println("Invalid Choice");
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SwitchStatementProgram obj = new SwitchStatementProgram();
        obj.menuExample();
    }
}




2. LOOPING STATEMENTS
Looping statements are used to repeat a block of code
until a specific condition becomes false.

**FOR LOOP**
The for loop is an entry-controlled loop.
It is used when the number of iterations is known in advance.

Real-world example:
Printing numbers or processing fixed data sets

class ForLoopProgram {
      void printNumbers() {
        for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
            System.out.println("Value: " + i);
        }
    }
public static void main(String[] args) {
        ForLoopProgram obj = new ForLoopProgram();
        obj.printNumbers();
    }
}

**WHILE LOOP**
The while loop is an entry-controlled loop.
The condition is checked before execution.
Important Point:
- Loop may execute zero times

Real-world example:
ATM withdrawal process


class WhileLoopProgram {

    void atmExample() {
        int balance = 4000;
        int withdrawAmount = 1000;

        while (balance >= withdrawAmount) {
            balance -= withdrawAmount;
            System.out.println("Remaining Balance: " + balance);
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        WhileLoopProgram obj = new WhileLoopProgram();
        obj.atmExample();
    }
}



**DO-WHILE LOOP**

The do-while loop is an exit-controlled loop.
It executes the loop body at least once.

Real-world example:
OTP verification

class DoWhileLoopProgram {

    void otpExample() {
        int attempt = 1;

        do {
            System.out.println("OTP Attempt: " + attempt);
            attempt++;
        } while (attempt <= 3);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        DoWhileLoopProgram obj = new DoWhileLoopProgram();
        obj.otpExample();
    }
}



3. JUMP STATEMENTS

Jump statements are used to transfer control
from one part of the program to another.

**BREAK STATEMENT**

The break statement is used to immediately
terminate a loop or switch statement.

class BreakStatementProgram {

    void breakExample() {
        for (int i = 1; i <= 10; i++) {
            if (i == 6) {
                break;
            }
            System.out.println(i);
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        BreakStatementProgram obj = new BreakStatementProgram();
        obj.breakExample();
    }
}



**CONTINUE STATEMENT**

The continue statement skips the current iteration
and continues with the next iteration of the loop.

class ContinueStatementProgram {

    void continueExample() {
        for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
            if (i == 3) {
                continue;
            }
            System.out.println(i);
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ContinueStatementProgram obj = new ContinueStatementProgram();
        obj.continueExample();
    }
}


**RETURN STATEMENT**

The return statement is used to end the execution
of a method and optionally return a value.

class ReturnStatementProgram {

    int add(int a, int b) {
        return a + b;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ReturnStatementProgram obj = new ReturnStatementProgram();
        int result = obj.add(10, 20);
        System.out.println("Sum: " + result);
    }
}
